Feature: slice-2 / next-intl-pl-en
Branch: feat/web-app-slice-2
Verified: 2026-05-27
Anchored to: docs/18 §14 (i18n contract)

What was built
==============
- `pnpm add next-intl` → 4.12.0.
- `marketing-site/messages/pl.json` + `en.json` — flat namespaces
  (metadata, scaffold, nav). Same key namespacing rule the doc
  prescribes: marketing-only keys live alongside; copy that overlaps
  with Flutter ARBs (auth.login_button, quota.warning_banner) will be
  added as those features arrive.
- `marketing-site/src/i18n/routing.ts` — single source of truth for
  locales + defaultLocale + localePrefix. `as-needed` mode picked
  per docs/18 §14.4 (PL primary market, no PL prefix).
- `marketing-site/src/i18n/request.ts` — getRequestConfig that loads
  the per-request locale's JSON. `hasLocale` guard falls back to the
  default if a bogus locale slips through.
- `marketing-site/src/proxy.ts` — locale routing. Renamed from
  `middleware.ts` per Next.js 16 convention (the rename is purely
  cosmetic; the function signature is identical). Build no longer
  emits the deprecation warning.
- `marketing-site/next.config.ts` — wires `createNextIntlPlugin` so
  the framework knows where the per-request config lives.
- `marketing-site/src/app/[locale]/layout.tsx` — locale-scoped root
  layout. Validates the param against `routing.locales` and 404s
  otherwise. Emits `<html lang={locale}>`, wires
  `<NextIntlClientProvider>`, and feeds hreflang alternates into
  `<head>` via Metadata.alternates.languages. Pre-renders both
  locale shells at build time via `generateStaticParams()` +
  `setRequestLocale()` (the supported Next.js 16 + next-intl 4 path
  for SSG with locale).
- `marketing-site/src/app/[locale]/page.tsx` — all strings come from
  `useTranslations("scaffold")` / `getTranslations`. Zero hard-coded
  copy.

How verified
============
1. `pnpm build` exit 0 — both /pl and /en shown in the route table
   as SSG pre-rendered:

     Route (app)
     ┌ ○ /_not-found
     └ ● /[locale]
       ├ /pl
       └ /en

     ƒ Proxy (Middleware)

2. `curl http://localhost:3000/` (no Accept-Language) and
   `curl http://localhost:3000/en` confirm:
     - PL: <html lang="pl">, title "Superwizor AI — drugi mózg
       superwizora", description in Polish, og:locale=pl_PL,
       body contains "Drugi mózg" + "Nagraj sesję".
     - EN: <html lang="en">, title "Superwizor AI — a
       supervisor's second brain", description in English,
       og:locale=en_US, body contains "supervisor" +
       "Record a session".
3. hreflang alternates emitted in <head> for BOTH locales:
     <link rel="alternate" hrefLang="pl" href="https://superwizor.ai"/>
     <link rel="alternate" hrefLang="en" href="https://superwizor.ai/en"/>
   Confirms search-engine indexability per docs/18 §14.4.
4. Puppeteer screenshots with explicit Accept-Language headers
   (snap.mjs accepts a 4th arg for this) — `home-pl-*.png` and
   `home-en-*.png` at three breakpoints each. Opened with Read tool,
   confirmed:
     - PL shows "Drugi mózg superwizora.", Polish body copy, CTA
       "ZAREJESTRUJ SIĘ" + "Zobacz plany", marker in PL.
     - EN shows "A supervisor's second brain.", English body copy,
       CTA "SIGN UP" + "See pricing", marker in EN.
     - All three breakpoints responsive at both locales with no
       clipping.
5. `preview_console_logs` (level=error) returns "No console logs" —
   the FORMATTING_ERROR found and fixed during build (next-intl's
   t() tried to interpolate {page} as a MessageFormat var; the
   title template is Next.js syntax, not next-intl syntax — fix:
   hard-code `template: "%s · Superwizor AI"` in code, drop it from
   messages).

Compliance with docs/18 §14
===========================
- ✓ Supported locales: pl (primary), en (secondary) — matches §14.1.
- ✓ Stack: next-intl on Next.js — §14.2.
- ✓ URL strategy: "as-needed" routing, PL no prefix, EN at /en — §14.4.
- ✓ hreflang alternate links on every page — §14.4.
- ✓ messages/{pl,en}.json — §14.2 file location.
- ✓ No hard-coded Polish strings in components — §14.5 backend
  strings policy.
- ☐ Locale resolution order (§14.3) — only step 1 (URL prefix) and
  step 4 (Accept-Language) are wired today. Steps 2 (user
  preference) + 3 (cookie) come in feature 4 (firebase-auth-init)
  and a follow-up; flagged but not blocking for this scaffold.
- ☐ check-l10n-parity.sh CI script (§14.2) — lands in Slice 6
  (i18n-polish-launch) per PROGRESS.md.

